      "prefix": "Chapter 5",
      "title": "The Table of Elements",
      "summary": "Over sixty chemical elements existed without any clear system. Dmitri Mendeleev wrote each element on an index card like cards in a game. He organized all elements into rows and columns in the Periodic Table.",
      "content": "The winter of 1869 was very cold. Dmitri Mendeleev had very little time. He was a professor at St. Petersburg University.\n\nHe needed to finish a chemistry book for his students. But there was a big problem. At that time chemistry was full of confusion.\n\nScientists knew more than sixty elements. Nobody understood how they worked together.\n\nThe old textbooks looked like phone books. Students had to learn long lists of weights and colors. There was no clear system in the books.\n\nMendeleev did not like this big mess. He believed that nature followed clear rules. He wanted to find the hidden pattern.\n\nSo Mendeleev made a card game for his work. He wrote the name and weight of each element on small cards.\n\nHe carried the cards in his coat pocket. When he had free time, he placed them on a table. He moved them like playing cards.\n\nFirst, he arranged the cards by weight. Then he grouped them by chemical features. Still, his attempts did not work.\n\nHe worked for three days without sleep. Mendeleev walked around his room and drank hot tea. He stared at his cards for hours.\n\nOn the afternoon of February 17, he was very tired. He fell asleep on his messy desk.\n\nIn his sleep, the confusion disappeared. Mendeleev later wrote about this moment in his journal:\n\n\"I saw a table in a dream. All the elements fell into the right place.\"\n\nHe woke up suddenly and took a clean sheet of paper. He quickly drew the table from his dream.\n\nHe put the elements in order of increasing weight. The chemical features returned in a regular rhythm. Similar elements sat together in straight columns.\n\nMendeleev made a very clever choice. When he saw an empty space, he did not panic. He did not force wrong facts into his table.\n\nInstead, he left empty boxes in the grid. He said that nature was not broken. Scientists simply needed to find the missing elements.\n\nHe even predicted the weights and colors of these unknown elements. Many other scientists laughed at his ideas.\n\nFifteen years later, researchers found three new elements: gallium, scandium, and germanium. Each new element matched Mendeleev's predictions.\n\nThe Periodic Table became an important map for science. Mendeleev gave chemistry order and clarity."
