      "prefix": "Chapter 13",
      "title": "The Power of Steam",
      "summary": "Animals and waterwheels could not power large growing factories. James Watt improved the steam engine with a separate condenser chamber. Steam power drove trains, ships, and the entire Industrial Revolution.",
      "content": "Before the eighteenth century, people used animal power and muscle for work. Horses pulled heavy wagons, and oxen worked in the fields. Sailors waited for weeks when the wind did not blow.\n\nThe biggest problem was underground. Water quickly filled the deep coal mines in Britain. Workers could not remove the water with simple buckets.\n\nIn 1712, Thomas Newcomen built a new machine. It was the first useful steam engine in the world. It pumped water all day, but it used too much coal.\n\nCold water sprayed inside the hot metal cylinder. The cold water cooled the metal very fast. Then the engine burned lots of coal to get hot again.\n\nIn 1764, James Watt inspected a small Newcomen engine. Watt made scientific tools in Scotland at the University of Glasgow. The broken little engine stopped after a few minutes.\n\n\"This machine wastes too much fuel,\" Watt said to his friends. He studied the engine and measured the heat for many months. He wanted to save coal and energy.\n\nIn May 1765, Watt took a walk across Glasgow Green. The weather was cold and quiet. Suddenly, a great idea came to his mind.\n\nSteam always moved into empty spaces. Watt decided to cool the steam in a separate metal container. The main cylinder could stay hot all the time.\n\n\"My idea is very simple,\" Watt said. \"The cylinder must stay hot, and the steam cools outside.\"\n\nThis clever idea changed the world of machines. Watt saved more than seventy percent of the coal. His new engine was strong and fast.\n\nWatt worked with Matthew Boulton, a businessman from Birmingham. Together, they added spinning gears to the engine. Soon, the engine did much more than pump water.\n\nBefore this, factories needed rushing water from rivers. Now, owners put Watt's engines in any city. They built new factories near coal, trains, and workers.\n\nSteam engines soon moved large ships and fast trains. They worked in big cotton mills and iron factories. James Watt changed human work and started the modern world."
