      "prefix": "Chapter 14",
      "title": "Power in Your Pocket",
      "summary": "Electricity only existed as brief, flashing sparks that vanished instantly. Alessandro Volta stacked disks of zinc, copper, and salty cloth. He invented the first electric battery and produced a steady current.",
      "content": "In the eighteenth century, electricity was only a party trick. Rich people watched bright sparks jump from glass jars. Guests laughed and jumped when the sparks shocked them.\n\nElectricity seemed wild and dangerous. It flashed for a second and then disappeared into the air. Nobody knew how to make it flow like water.\n\nIn 1780, an Italian doctor named Luigi Galvani worked in Bologna. He cut open a dead frog on a wooden table. His assistant held a metal knife near the frog's leg.\n\nAn electric machine made a small spark nearby. Suddenly, the dead frog's leg kicked hard. Galvani was very surprised and tested the frog many times.\n\nGalvani believed that animals produced their own electricity. He called this force animal electricity. Other scientists read his reports with great interest.\n\nIn northern Italy, a scientist named Alessandro Volta studied the experiment. At first, Volta liked Galvani's ideas. Then he noticed an important detail about the metals.\n\nThe frog kicked only when two different metals touched its wet leg. The assistant used both copper and zinc. Volta believed that the frog did not make any power.\n\n\"The animal only shows the electric shock,\" Volta said. \"The power comes from two different wet metals.\"\n\nVolta wanted to prove his idea without using animals. In 1799, he built a small tower on his work table. He placed discs of zinc and silver in a tall pile.\n\nBetween the metal pieces, he put cardboard with salty water. Then he connected two wires to the top and bottom. Volta touched the wires with his bare fingers.\n\nHe felt a steady electric stream in his hands. The power did not stop after one spark. He called this invention the voltaic pile.\n\nIt was the first real battery in the world. For the first time, scientists had steady electricity. In 1800, Volta shared his battery with other scientists.\n\nSoon, chemists used batteries to separate water into gases. They also discovered new metals with this power. Volta's battery changed science and technology forever.\n\nToday, batteries power our phones, watches, and electric cars. Modern life started with that small pile of wet metal."
