      "content": "In the late nineteenth century, big cities had a dirty problem. Thousands of horses crowded the streets of London and New York. The animals left piles of waste, and the air smelled terrible.\n\nBig steam trains traveled between cities on metal tracks. But these large trains could not enter narrow city streets. People still needed horses for daily travel.\n\nA German engineer named Karl Benz wanted a better solution. He planned a small vehicle with a new kind of engine. The fuel burned inside the engine cylinder to create power.\n\nBenz worked in his shop in the city of Mannheim. He built a light vehicle with three metal wheels. It had a small steering handle and a steel frame.\n\nThe engine used ligroin, a liquid from local medicine shops. In 1885, Benz drove his vehicle through the quiet streets. It moved at sixteen kilometers per hour.\n\nBenz patented his motor car in 1886. People on the street felt afraid of the noisy smoke. Some people even laughed and called the machine dangerous.\n\nBenz felt sad and locked the vehicle in his workshop. He did not want to show it to the public. His brave wife Bertha Benz had a bold plan.\n\nOne morning in August 1888, Bertha woke up before sunrise. She quietly pushed the motor car away with her two sons. Her husband was still asleep in bed.\n\nBertha started the engine and drove toward her mother's town. The long journey was more than one hundred kilometers. She wanted to prove that the car was useful.\n\nDuring the trip, Bertha fixed many small problems. She bought more fuel at a village pharmacy. Then she cleaned a small pipe with her long hatpin.\n\nShe also fixed a broken wire with her clothing. In another town, a shoemaker added leather to the wooden brakes. The car worked well again.\n\nBertha and her sons arrived in Pforzheim before sunset. She sent a happy message to her husband by wire. Newspapers soon told the exciting story across Europe.\n\nHer trip showed people that cars were ready for daily life. Soon, many customers ordered vehicles from Benz's factory.\n\nCars quickly replaced horses on city streets. Karl Benz built the machine, but Bertha drove it into history."
