      "summary": "Humans dreamed of soaring like birds through the skies for centuries. The Wright brothers realized that the pilot must actively control the wings. In 1903, they made the first controlled, powered airplane flight in history.",
      "content": "For thousands of years, people looked at birds and dreamed of flying. In the nineteenth century, hot air balloons floated in the sky. But the wind pushed them, and pilots could not steer.\n\nPeople wanted a machine with an engine that could fly anywhere. Many famous scientists said this was completely impossible. Early flying machines were heavy and crashed into rivers.\n\nIn Dayton, Ohio, two brothers had a small bicycle shop. Their names were Wilbur and Orville Wright. They watched birds turn and balance in the strong wind.\n\n\"Flying is not only about engine power,\" Wilbur said. \"The pilot must control the wings.\"\n\nThe brothers knew that flying was like riding a bicycle. A rider must make quick movements to stay balanced. A pilot needed to control the airplane in the air.\n\nWilbur and Orville built a small wind box in their workshop. They tested more than two hundred wing shapes inside the box. They found many mistakes in old science books.\n\nThe brothers designed wooden wings that could twist. The pilot moved wires with his body to tilt the wings. Now the airplane could turn safely in the sky.\n\nIn December 1903, the brothers took their airplane to Kitty Hawk. This place in North Carolina had sandy hills and strong winds. The wooden plane was called the Flyer.\n\nOn December 17, cold ocean winds blew across the sand. Orville lay down flat on the lower wooden wing. The two wooden propellers began to spin with a loud roar.\n\nThe Flyer moved forward along a wooden track. Then it lifted into the cold air. It flew for twelve seconds and landed softly on the sand.\n\nThe brothers flew three more times that morning. On the last flight, Wilbur stayed in the air for fifty-nine seconds. He traveled more than two hundred and fifty meters.\n\nThe Wright brothers solved the puzzle of flight. Only sixty-six years later, humans walked on the Moon. The sky became a new road for the entire world."
