      "content": "In the fifteenth century, a single book cost a lot of money. It could cost as much as an entire farm.\n\nPeople copied every page by hand. Monks sat in cold stone rooms for months. They wrote Latin words on animal skins with feather pens.\n\nThis work was very slow. Because of this, Europe had very few books.\n\nKings and rich churches protected their precious books. They chained the heavy books to wooden desks.\n\nFor ordinary people, books did not exist. Knowledge stayed with kings and priests. New ideas traveled very slowly.\n\nJohannes Gutenberg wanted to change this situation. He was a goldsmith in the German city of Mainz. He spent years on a secret and expensive project.\n\nGutenberg knew about wooden blocks. Cutting letters into wood took too much time. Also, the soft wood broke under strong pressure.\n\nSo Gutenberg chose metal. He cut clear letters onto hard steel pieces. Then he hit them into softer copper bars.\n\nThese marks in the copper became molds. Gutenberg melted lead, tin, and antimony together. He poured the hot metal into the molds.\n\nIn this way, he made thousands of identical metal letters. These small metal blocks lasted for a long time. Workers could use them again and again.\n\nA printer could arrange the letters into words and print a page. Then he could sort the letters to make a new page.\n\nNext, Gutenberg solved the problem of ink. Normal water ink slid off metal letters. It made dark spots on the paper.\n\nGutenberg mixed oil with black soot. This made a thick and sticky paste. The dark ink stayed on the metal letters and printed clean words.\n\nGutenberg needed a machine to press the paper. He changed a heavy wooden press from a wine farm. A worker pulled a wooden lever and printed a page in seconds.\n\nAround the year 1455, Gutenberg showed his famous work. It was a large Bible in Latin. People looked at the clean pages and were amazed.\n\nThe new machine spread quickly across Europe. In fifty years, workshops opened in more than two hundred cities. Printers made twenty million books.\n\nGutenberg's letters of lead changed the world. Now new ideas about science and medicine traveled fast. Thousands of people could read and learn."
