      "summary": "Sending a letter across the Atlantic Ocean took weeks or months by ship. Samuel Morse invented the electric telegraph and a code of dots and dashes. Undersea cables allowed instant messages to cross oceans in seconds.",
      "content": "For thousands of years, messages traveled very slowly. A message could only go as fast as a running horse.\n\nPeople carried all news with great effort. Letters traveled on rough dirt roads by horse. Wooden sailing ships carried letters across stormy seas.\n\nA government in London started a war. Weeks passed before people in New York knew. Families waited months for news from across the sea.\n\nSome countries built towers on hills with large wooden arms. Workers made signals from one valley to another. Rain, thick fog, and dark nights easily blocked these signals.\n\nIn 1825, an American painter named Samuel Morse suffered from this slow communication.\n\nMorse was painting a portrait in Washington. A messenger on a horse gave him a letter from his father.\n\nThe letter brought terrible news. His young wife was very sick in Connecticut.\n\nMorse quickly packed his bags and rode north on his horse. He traveled through the dust for four hard days.\n\nWhen he arrived home, he was too late. His dear wife was already dead. Her grave was in the churchyard.\n\nMorse felt deep sadness and made a promise. He wanted to find a way to send thoughts across long distances instantly.\n\nSeven years later, Morse was traveling on a ship from Europe. A passenger showed Morse a magnet and spoke about electricity. Electric current traveled through wires very fast.\n\nMorse had a great idea. A person could tap a switch to start and stop electric current.\n\nThis created signals at the other end of the wire. The signals traveled without delay.\n\nMorse worked with a clever mechanic named Alfred Vail. They made a simple code with dots and dashes.\n\nCommon letters like *E* had only one dot. So messages were quick to send.\n\nOn May 24, 1844, Morse sat in a room in the United States Capitol. He tapped a small brass key. A wire connected his machine to the city of Baltimore forty miles away.\n\nSeconds later, the machine in Baltimore began to click. It printed the famous words: \"What hath God wrought.\"\n\nThe electric telegraph changed everything. Cables crossed large lands and the cold floor of the Atlantic Ocean. People across the world could now talk to each other in real time."
