      "title": "The Tiny Switch",
      "summary": "Early computers filled whole rooms and used thousands of fragile glass tubes. Bell Labs scientists invented the solid-state silicon transistor. Silicon microchips with billions of transistors made modern computers real.",
      "content": "In the 1940s, the most powerful computer in the world was huge. It filled a large room and produced a lot of heat.\n\nPeople called this machine the ENIAC. It used seventeen thousand glowing glass tubes. These glass tubes controlled the electricity and solved difficult math problems.\n\nBut the glass tubes caused many problems. They used too much electricity and broke very often.\n\nEvery day, workers crawled inside the hot machine. They searched for broken glass tubes and changed them.\n\nEngineers at Bell Laboratories in New Jersey wanted a better way. William Shockley, John Bardeen, and Walter Brattain searched for a solid solution.\n\nThey wanted to control electric currents inside solid crystals. They tested materials like germanium and silicon. These crystals did not need glass bulbs.\n\nOn December 16, 1947, Bardeen and Brattain did a test in their laboratory.\n\nThey placed two small pieces of gold onto a piece of germanium. They sent a weak electric signal through one piece of gold. A much stronger current came out of the other piece.\n\nThe test was a great success. The scientists invented the transistor. This tiny switch could make signals stronger and control electricity.\n\nThe new switch changed the world of technology. It had no delicate glass parts and produced very little heat. It used very little power and worked very fast.\n\nSoon, Shockley improved the design. Factories made millions of these reliable switches.\n\nBy the late 1950s, engineers made another big discovery. They placed many transistors onto one small piece of silicon. They created the microchip.\n\nToday, the transistor is the most common object in history. Billions of tiny switches fit on a silicon chip smaller than a stamp.\n\nOur modern world needs this tiny switch. It guided astronauts to the Moon and helped doctors in hospitals. Now it powers small computers in the hands of billions of people."
