      "summary": "Computers at different universities could not easily share information. Tim Berners-Lee at CERN created HTML code and simple web links. He gave the World Wide Web to everyone for free and connected humanity.",
      "content": "In 1989, finding information on the early internet was very difficult. It was like searching for one book in a dark library.\n\nComputers could send information over telephone lines. But the systems were slow and confusing. Only computer scientists understood the complex screens and number codes.\n\nDifferent computer brands did not share files easily. Important scientific discoveries stayed on separate computers.\n\nTim Berners-Lee was a British computer scientist. He worked at the CERN laboratory in Switzerland.\n\nThousands of researchers from many countries worked at CERN. They wasted valuable time because their machines could not talk to each other.\n\nIn March 1989, Berners-Lee wrote a plan for his manager. On the paper, his boss wrote famous words: \"Vague but exciting...\"\n\nBerners-Lee wanted to build a simple system. It used links inside texts. A user could click a link and jump to another document immediately.\n\nBerners-Lee worked in his office on a black computer. He created three important tools for the web.\n\nFirst, he invented HTML. This code allowed computers to show text, pictures, and links.\n\nNext, he created HTTP. This was a set of rules to send web pages over networks.\n\nFinally, he created the URL. This gave every page on the internet its own unique address.\n\nIn 1991, the World Wide Web was ready for the public. Then Berners-Lee and CERN made a great decision.\n\nThey gave the code to the entire world for free. Anyone could make a website or open a browser without paying money.\n\nThis generous choice changed human communication forever. The web changed business, news, and how friends talk to each other. With one simple click, people connected across the entire globe."
